  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nIssued 250 AM MDT FRI OCT 23 2026\nThe short-wave ridge that kept the weekend dry will begin to\nbreak down Monday, as another low digs down the coast of \nBritish Columbia and into the northwest CONUS. Uncertainty \nremains on the exact timing of when this low will impact our \narea. Ensemble mean 500mb heights have it passing our area late \nMonday into Tuesday, which is reflected in the forecast with \nincreasing precipitation chances Monday afternoon, peaking \novernight Monday. The best chances of precipitation will come \nacross higher terrain of SW-Idaho, with a 55-65% chance in \nValley County, elsewhere north of the Snake Plain will carry a \n30-50% chance. With this system bringing cooler temperatures and\nlowering snow levels, elevations above 6.5 kft MSL have a \n35-60% chance of seeing at least an inch of snow between 6pm MDT\nMonday through 6pm MDT Tuesday; elevations above 7kft MSL have \na 30-50% chance of seeing greater than 2 inches over the same \nperiod. Uncertainty increases beyond Tuesday with the pattern \nevolution. However, with the Grand Ensemble resolving zonal flow\nbefore general troughing, a cool and unsettled pattern is \nlikely to continue. The CPC outlooks seem to agree, with our \narea highlighted with near normal temperatures and leaning \ntowards above normal in regard to precipitation.",
